//! crypto/secp256k1_scalarmul.rye — Season G (Cryptography): scalar multiplication on secp256k1 authored in pure Rye, composed entirely over
//! the GREEN group law (crypto/secp256k1_group.rye), which itself stands over the GREEN base field (crypto/fe_secp256k1.rye). ECDSA over
//! secp256k1 — the signature scheme Bitcoin and Ethereum sign with — computes the public key Q = d·G and, on every verification, the two
//! products u1·G and u2·Q; this file authors exactly that operation, k·P, as a composition over the group's two primitives, point doubling
//! and point addition. It adds no new field math and no new group math; it is the tractable, highest-Lindy move that turns a proven group
//! into a proven scalar multiplication — the last arithmetic rung before ECDSA verification and public-key recovery can be assembled, the
//! exact sibling of crypto/ed25519_scalarmul.rye over the edwards25519 group.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, parity-checked, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/20260816-022000_secp256k1-group-law-the-proven-curve.md (the group law this composes over, whose closing
//! GREEN line names scalar multiplication as its next composition) and
//! active-designing/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (the priority spine: Rye first, Glow on green Rye).
//! Kin: crypto/secp256k1_group.rye (the group this composes over, GREEN) · crypto/fe_secp256k1.rye (the base field beneath it, GREEN) ·
//! crypto/ed25519_scalarmul.rye (the sibling ladder over the twisted Edwards group). secp256k1 is not in Monocypher, so its source parity is
//! not a rung here; the reference known-answers plus Zig's independent std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1 are the two witnesses.
//!
//! THE CRUX — a scalar k times a point P is P added to itself k times, which the double-and-add ladder computes in a number of group
//! operations proportional to the bit length of k rather than to k itself: walk the scalar's 256 bits from most significant to least, and at
//! each bit double the running accumulator, then add P when that bit is set. The accumulator starts at the group identity, the point at
//! infinity (X = 0, Y = 1, Z = 0). Because the complete addition and doubling formulas beneath are exception-free, the ladder needs no
//! special case for the identity accumulator or for adding a point to itself. The one product with the outside world is the scalar's
//! little-endian byte order, the order this file and the sibling Ed25519 ladder both walk; the ecosystem's big-endian scalar convention is a
//! caller's byte reversal, not the ladder's concern.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device, no identity. A scalar and a curve point are arithmetic,
//! not a secret and not a signature: this file signs nothing, holds no key, and calls no std.crypto in the primitive. ECDSA's signing with an
//! identity key (Q = d·G for a real private d) stays its own separate, gated round; this rung only proves that k·P is computed correctly.
//! This double-and-add ladder branches on the scalar's bits and so is deliberately VARIABLE-TIME — correct, not yet timing-safe; a
//! constant-time ladder for secret scalars stays a separate named horizon, the same horizon the whole Season carries. Parity is proven at
//! selftest against two references runnable now — algebraic known-answers anyone can check (0·G is the point at infinity, 1·G is G, 2·G is the
//! doubling of G, (k+1)·G is k·G plus G), and Zig's own std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1.mul as an independent implementation, compared byte-for-byte
//! on the affine coordinates of k·P across a spread of scalars and across both the base point and one of its multiples.
//!
//! Run from the repository root:
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/secp256k1_scalarmul.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const fe = @import("fe_secp256k1.rye");
const pt = @import("secp256k1_group.rye");

pub const secp256k1_scalarmul_version = "20260816.023000";

/// The maximum number of scalar bits the ladder walks — a full little-endian 256-bit scalar, the width secp256k1 scalars occupy. Named so the
/// loop bound is a stated constant rather than a literal buried in the walk.
pub const scalar_bits: u32 = 256;

/// The group identity in homogeneous projective coordinates: the point at infinity, x = X/Z with Z = 0. Carried as (X = 0, Y = 1, Z = 0), the
/// neutral element the double-and-add accumulator starts from — the complete formulas add it and double it with no special case.
pub fn identity() pt.Point {
    const r = pt.Point{
        .x = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 } },
        .y = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 } },
        .z = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 } },
    };
    // invariant: the identity carries Z = 0 exactly — the point at infinity, not merely some finite point.
    assert(r.z.l[0] == 0 and r.y.l[0] == 1);
    return r;
}

/// Scalar multiplication k·P by the double-and-add ladder: walk the 32-byte little-endian scalar s from its most significant bit down to its
/// least, doubling the accumulator at every bit and adding P wherever the bit is set. Composes only pt.dbl and pt.add over the GREEN group
/// law — no field inversion, no new curve math. Deliberately variable-time (it branches on scalar bits); constant-time is the named horizon.
pub fn scalarmul(p: pt.Point, s: *const [32]u8) pt.Point {
    // invariant: the ladder walks exactly scalar_bits bits, high to low — the bound is a named constant, not an open loop.
    assert(scalar_bits == 256);
    var r = identity();
    var i: u32 = scalar_bits;
    while (i > 0) {
        i -= 1;
        // invariant: the byte index stays inside the 32-byte scalar (i < 256 so i >> 3 < 32), and the bit index fits three bits.
        assert((i >> 3) < 32 and (i & 7) <= 7);
        r = pt.dbl(r);
        const byte = s[i >> 3];
        const bit = (byte >> @intCast(i & 7)) & 1;
        if (bit == 1) {
            r = pt.add(r, p);
        }
    }
    // invariant: the result is a well-formed projective point — every coordinate limb stays a canonical 32-bit value the group law maintains.
    assert(r.z.l[0] <= 0xffffffff and r.y.l[0] <= 0xffffffff);
    return r;
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Build a 32-byte little-endian scalar from a small unsigned value — the low eight bytes carry v, the rest are zero. Used to name small
/// known-answer scalars (1, 2, 5, …) plainly inside the selftest.
fn le32(v: u64) [32]u8 {
    var s = [_]u8{0} ** 32;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    var acc = v;
    while (i < 8) : (i += 1) {
        s[i] = @intCast(acc & 0xff);
        acc >>= 8;
    }
    return s;
}

/// Independent reference: Zig's own std.crypto secp256k1, used ONLY inside the selftest as a second implementation to prove our ladder matches
/// byte-for-byte. The primitive above never calls it.
const ZSec = std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1;

/// Copy a Zig point into our representation through the canonical byte form of each projective coordinate, so parity compares two genuinely
/// independent implementations on the same projective representative — the same bridge the group law's selftest uses.
fn from_zig(zp: ZSec) pt.Point {
    const xb = zp.x.toBytes(.big);
    const yb = zp.y.toBytes(.big);
    const zb = zp.z.toBytes(.big);
    return pt.Point{ .x = fe.from_bytes(&xb), .y = fe.from_bytes(&yb), .z = fe.from_bytes(&zb) };
}

/// True when our point reads to the same affine (x, y) as the Zig point — the parity check for a non-identity result.
fn affine_eq(p: pt.Point, zp: ZSec) bool {
    const za = zp.affineCoordinates();
    return bytes_eq(&pt.affine_x(p), &za.x.toBytes(.big)) and bytes_eq(&pt.affine_y(p), &za.y.toBytes(.big));
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const b = ZSec.basePoint;
    const pb = from_zig(b);

    // 1. Algebraic known-answers anyone can check.
    {
        // 0·G is the point at infinity — its Z coordinate packs to zero.
        var zero_b = [_]u8{0} ** 32;
        if (!bytes_eq(&fe.to_bytes(scalarmul(pb, &le32(0)).z), &zero_b)) return error.ZeroScalarMismatch;

        // 1·G is G itself.
        if (!affine_eq(scalarmul(pb, &le32(1)), b)) return error.OneScalarMismatch;

        // 2·G is the doubling of G.
        if (!bytes_eq(&pt.affine_x(scalarmul(pb, &le32(2))), &pt.affine_x(pt.dbl(pb)))) return error.TwoScalarMismatch;
        if (!bytes_eq(&pt.affine_y(scalarmul(pb, &le32(2))), &pt.affine_y(pt.dbl(pb)))) return error.TwoScalarYMismatch;

        // (k+1)·G equals k·G + G, for a small k — the ladder is consistent with a single extra addition.
        const k: u64 = 20;
        const kb = scalarmul(pb, &le32(k));
        if (!bytes_eq(&pt.affine_x(scalarmul(pb, &le32(k + 1))), &pt.affine_x(pt.add(kb, pb)))) return error.SuccessorMismatch;
    }

    // 2. Independent-implementation parity: our ladder equals Zig's std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1.mul byte-for-byte on the affine coordinates of
    //    k·P across a spread of scalars, over both the base point and one of its multiples (3·G, still a prime-order-subgroup point).
    {
        const small = [_]u64{ 1, 2, 3, 7, 16, 255, 1000, 65537 };
        // A fixed large scalar with a small top byte — well under the group order, never zero, so Zig's mul rejects nothing.
        const big = [32]u8{
            0x9d, 0xb6, 0x1c, 0x0f, 0x27, 0x44, 0x8e, 0x11, 0xa3, 0x5c, 0x02, 0x77, 0xd8, 0x41, 0x63, 0x9a,
            0x0e, 0x5b, 0xc7, 0x30, 0x88, 0x12, 0x6f, 0xa9, 0x44, 0xd1, 0x3e, 0x07, 0x91, 0x2a, 0x60, 0x0e,
        };

        const b3 = b.dbl().add(b); // 3·G, an independent prime-order point to multiply as well as the base point.
        const points = [_]ZSec{ b, b3 };

        for (points) |zp| {
            const p = from_zig(zp);
            for (small) |v| {
                const s = le32(v);
                if (!affine_eq(scalarmul(p, &s), try zp.mul(s, .little))) return error.RefSmallScalarMismatch;
            }
            if (!affine_eq(scalarmul(p, &big), try zp.mul(big, .little))) return error.RefBigScalarMismatch;
        }
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-secp256k1-scalarmul: scalar multiplication on secp256k1 authored in pure Rye — Season G's composition over the GREEN " ++
        "group law (crypto/secp256k1_group.rye) and, beneath it, the GREEN base field (crypto/fe_secp256k1.rye). k·P is computed by the " ++
        "double-and-add ladder: walk the 32-byte little-endian scalar from its high bit down, double the accumulator at every bit and add P " ++
        "where the bit is set, starting from the group identity, the point at infinity (X = 0, Y = 1, Z = 0). Because the complete addition " ++
        "and doubling formulas beneath are exception-free, the ladder needs no special case for the identity accumulator or for adding a " ++
        "point to itself. It composes only point doubling and point addition — no field inversion, no new curve math. The ladder is " ++
        "deliberately variable-time (it branches on scalar bits), so it is correct but not yet timing-safe; a constant-time ladder for secret " ++
        "scalars stays a separate named horizon. The primitive calls no std.crypto. Parity holds against two references runnable now: " ++
        "algebraic known-answers (0·G is the point at infinity, 1·G is G, 2·G is the doubling of G, (k+1)·G is k·G plus G), and our authored " ++
        "Rye equals Zig's independent std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1.mul byte-for-byte on the affine coordinates of k·P across a spread of scalars " ++
        "over both the base point and 3·G. secp256k1 is not in Monocypher, so the two witnesses are the reference known-answers and Zig's " ++
        "std.crypto. Purely local arithmetic — a scalar and a point are not a secret and not a signature, so no real key, no signing, no " ++
        "network, no funds, no real device; ECDSA's signing with an identity key stays its own separate, gated round. The group now " ++
        "multiplies by a scalar, and ECDSA verification (u1·G + u2·Q) and public-key recovery become compositions over this ladder — the " ++
        "reach the Bitcoin/Ethereum address arc was always climbing toward.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
